\DOC SIMD_SIMPLIFY_TAC

\TYPE {SIMD_SIMPLIFY_TAC : thm list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Rewrites assumptions equating a machine-word read with a nested bit-vector
expression into a simplified form, collapsing {word_subword}/{word_join}
lane-extraction chains and unfolding supplied definitions.

\DESCRIBE
The argument {unfold_defs : thm list} is a list of equational theorems whose
left-hand sides are expanded while the assumptions are simplified; in the
observed traces it is always a single-element list. The tactic touches only the
assumption list: an assumption of the form {t = u} (a register or memory read
equated with a word expression) may be replaced by a simplified equation, the
old assumption being removed and the new one appended at the end, so the
ordering of the surrounding assumptions can shift; in other applications no
assumption changes and the goal is returned unaltered.
{
           A ?- t
   ======================  SIMD_SIMPLIFY_TAC unfold_defs
           A' ?- t
}
\noindent Here {A'} is {A} with zero or more word-equation assumptions
rewritten and moved to the end.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this behaviour: the tactic never splits or closes
a goal, and every input is a single machine-level {eventually} goal carrying a
long list of read-equation assumptions.

\ENDDOC
